## 87. Analogy as Graph

Represent each concept as a node.

```text
[Bird] ──lives in──> [Nest]

[Lion] ──lives in──> [Den]
```

The task is to find another edge with the same relationship.

Formally:

```text
(Bird,Nest,R)
```

must correspond to:

```text
(Lion,Den,R)
```

---

## 88. Relationship Vector

Represent a relation as:

```text
R=(direction,type,strength,domain)
```

Example:

```text
Doctor → Hospital
```

could be:

```text
R = (
  direction = forward,
  type = workplace,
  domain = profession,
  strength = direct
)
```

Then:

```text
Teacher + R = School
```

---

## 89. Semantic Distance

A good analogy has approximately equal relational structure:

```text
d(A,B)\approx d(C,D)
```

Here \(d\) does not necessarily mean physical distance. It can mean **semantic relationship**.

Example:

```text
Bird : Nest
Lion : Den
```

Both represent:

```text
Animal → Natural/typical shelter
```

---

## 90. Structural Analogy

Two pairs can have completely different objects but the same structure.

```text
Pen : Writing
Knife : Cutting
```

Structure:

```text
Tool → Function
```

The actual objects differ, but the relation is identical.

---

## 91. Relationship Direction Matrix

| Pair | Direction |
|---|---|
| Bird → Nest | Animal → Home |
| Nest → Bird | Home → Animal |
| Pen → Writing | Tool → Function |
| Writing → Pen | Function → Tool |
| Fire → Smoke | Cause → Effect |
| Smoke → Fire | Effect → Cause |
| Wheel → Car | Part → Whole |
| Car → Wheel | Whole → Part |

---

## 92. Common Exam Relationships

Memorize these high-frequency patterns:

```text
Animal → Home
Animal → Young one
Animal → Sound
Animal → Group
Object → Function
Tool → User
Worker → Workplace
Worker → Tool
Country → Capital
Country → Currency
State → Capital
Material → Product
Part → Whole
Cause → Effect
Disease → Symptom
Organ → Function
Instrument → Measurement
Person → Profession
Person → Discovery
Person → Invention
Word → Synonym
Word → Antonym
Number → Operation
Letter → Position
```

---

## 93. Animal Sound Analogy

```text
Dog : Bark :: Cat : Meow
```

Examples:

```text
Cow → Moo
Lion → Roar
Horse → Neigh
Snake → Hiss
Crow → Caw
```

---

## 94. Animal Group Analogy

```text
Bee : Swarm :: Lion : Pride
```

Examples:

```text
Fish → School
Wolf → Pack
Cow → Herd
Bird → Flock
```

---

## 95. Disease → Symptom

```text
Malaria : Fever :: Allergy : Sneezing
```

Use factual knowledge carefully.

---

## 96. Person → Profession

```text
Einstein : Physicist :: Darwin : Biologist
```

---

## 97. Person → Discovery

Examples may involve:

```text
Scientist → Discovery
Scientist → Theory
Inventor → Invention
```

These questions test general knowledge as well as reasoning.

---

## 98. Country → Capital

```text
India : New Delhi :: Japan : Tokyo
```

---

## 99. Country → Currency

```text
India : Rupee :: Japan : Yen
```

---

## 100. State → Capital

```text
Haryana : Chandigarh :: Rajasthan : Jaipur
```

---

## 101. Common Trap: Similar but Not Equivalent

Example:

```text
Doctor : Hospital :: Teacher : Student
```

Both are related, but not by the same relation.

Correct:

```text
Doctor : Hospital :: Teacher : School
```

Because:

```text
profession → workplace
```

---

## 102. Common Trap: Broad Relationship

Suppose:

```text
Bird : Nest
```

Possible choices:

```text
A. Dog : Kennel
B. Dog : Animal
C. Dog : Bone
D. Dog : Food
```

A is strongest because:

```text
Bird → home
Dog → home
```

---
